#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5dac73-ec22-5f59-b6b4-5af467826122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294401} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef6dd2-41b9-5ce9-a150-e2e384cdbc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294402} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b707c4-8e0c-5a96-87b0-7024112b8190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294403} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4785b1-9940-5c81-bfb9-d993312c70d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294404} {:answer/id #uuid "db86223e-0460-570a-80e8-13ae73d77628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db86223e-0460-570a-80e8-13ae73d77628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294405} :answer-field/id #uuid "c95bab29-2e85-5424-9339-70f5b63e00c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ea253-fa70-53c3-a318-c123823ef429" :question/math-academy-id "q-81063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(1)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(1)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}.\n$$"}]}
